{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792935403,"nonce":"e72ybAX/etB9NPvsCvP+df/LeCY=","title":"A guest error in a function Haxe holds aborts instead of reaching its catch","message":"ChimpMonk's engine calls each registered system as `try s.fn(this, dt)\ncatch (e:Dynamic) {...}`. A Wren Fn, Lua function or Python function\nregistered from that language with `addSystem` (parameter typed `(GameScene,\nFloat) -\u003e Void`) aborted the whole process when it raised, in both interp and\nhybrid modes: \"[ash] uncaught exception: String ...\" then \"hlp_throw called\nwithout active trap; aborting\". Reproduced in ChimpMonk's example/guests and in\ncaribou's systems fixture, where the error skipped the Haxe catch and surfaced\nat the host's outer trap.\n\nCause: an argument crossing into a Haxe call went through\nproto::value_to_dyn with only its kind, so a function bound for a function\ntype became caribou's var-args closure (`hlp_make_var_args` over a C entry).\nAsh's interpreter calls such an entry directly in CallClosure, without the\n`run_with_hl_trap` its record-closure branch and every native call use, so\nthe entry's `hlp_throw` found no trap for the interpreted try.\n\nCaribou's fix: the dispatcher's kinds carry each argument's type, and a\nfunction bound for a function type becomes a record closure of that type\n(callback::function_for_typed), which the interpreter calls under a trap and\na call site reaches by its signature. A function crossing into a Dynamic place\nstill becomes the var-args closure; Ash's interpreter gap is ash-7d's, being\nfixed there (reported 2026-10-25).","files":null}]}